BODY = '''<p>"Comfort height" is a maker's label, not a building-code term, so the number behind it depends on who is measuring. This page lines up three published sources: Kohler's definition of chair height, the ADA range for toilet seats, and a joint replacement clinic's rule for how high a seat should be for you.</p>

<h2>What Chair Height Means</h2>
<p>Kohler's toilet buying guide says toilet height is measured from the floor to the top of the seat. It says most toilets fall between 15 and 19 inches, with standard toilets under 17 inches and chair-height toilets at 17 inches or more. Kohler describes a chair-height toilet as approximately 2 inches higher than a standard one, which makes standing and sitting easier, and says many are ADA-compliant. A standard height toilet, in its words, is an ideal choice for people of average or smaller stature.</p>

<h2>The ADA Number</h2>
<p>The 2010 ADA Standards, section 604.4, say the seat height of a water closet shall be 17 inches minimum and 19 inches maximum above the finish floor, measured to the top of the seat. The ADA governs public and commercial buildings, not private homes, so it is a reference point for your own bathroom and not a rule. It does show that "taller" has a limit: a toilet above 19 inches is outside the range.</p>

<h2>How High Should Yours Be?</h2>
<p>The Joint Replacement Resource Centre, part of the Winnipeg Regional Health Authority, gives a rule based on your body. When you sit on the toilet, your feet should be on the floor and ideally your hips should be higher than your knees, on a slope of about 2 inches. If your knees are higher than your hips, it is much harder to get off the toilet. If your hips and knees are level, a raised seat of 2 to 3 inches may work.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Measure the toilet.</strong> The handout says to measure from the floor to the top of the toilet bowl, without the seat or lid.</li>
<li><strong>Find your height.</strong> Sit on a bed or chair where your feet are flat and your hips are about 2 inches above your knees, and measure from the floor to the seat.</li>
<li><strong>Subtract.</strong> The raised seat height is your total height minus the toilet height. The handout's example is 19 inches minus 15 inches, which gives a 4-inch seat.</li>
</ol>
<p class="note"><strong>Check which way a number was measured.</strong> Kohler measures to the top of the seat. The Winnipeg handout measures to the bowl with the seat off. A 17-inch figure from one is not the same height as a 17-inch figure from the other. Our <a href="/tools/raised-toilet-seat-height-calculator/">raised toilet seat height calculator</a> follows the handout's method.</p>

<h2>New Toilet or Raised Seat?</h2>
<p>MedlinePlus says raising the toilet seat height can help prevent falls, and suggests a raised seat or a commode chair. A raised seat adds height to the toilet you already have. A chair-height toilet changes the toilet itself, which suits a bathroom you are renovating. Our <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seat guide</a> compares five seats, and the <a href="/articles/toilet-safety-frame-raised-seat-or-grab-bar/">article on frames, seats and grab bars</a> covers the other ways to make standing up easier.</p>

<h2>Comfort Height Questions</h2>
<h3>Is comfort height the same as ADA height?</h3>
<p>Not always. Kohler says chair-height toilets measure 17 inches or more and many are ADA-compliant. The ADA range is 17 to 19 inches to the top of the seat, so look for the maker's listed seat height and not just the words "comfort height."</p>
<h3>Is a taller toilet right for everyone?</h3>
<p>Kohler says standard height toilets suit people of average or smaller stature. If you have had a hip or knee replacement, ask your surgeon or therapist which seat height they want, because the right height depends on your recovery.</p>
<h3>Does a taller toilet replace a grab bar?</h3>
<p>No. A higher seat shortens the distance you stand up, and a bar or frame gives your hands something to push on. See <a href="/articles/where-to-put-grab-bars/">where to put grab bars</a>.</p>'''

